A||||9||Joe Trainer||Torque converter lockup solenoid||||5||
Z||000000||Joe Trainer||02-21-2000||01:06 PM||||Heard alot about disconnecting the torque converter lockup solenoid to eliminate (troubleshoot) the problems associated with stalling while stopping and having a warm engine.  What problems will arise, if any, if the solenoid is permanently disconnected?  Thanks in advance...this is a forum.  Joe||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-21-2000||01:37 PM||||the tranny will run hotter which is likely to shorten it's life.<P>the gas milage will drop.<P>There is no benefit at all to leaving it disabled. It can be fixed in the driveway with common hand tools in a couple hours at most. Part cost is under 20$us according to notes I have. Haven't ordered it recently. Fiero Store has them if you don't want to fool with a GM/Pontiac dealer.<P>The solinoid lives under the side cover on the tranny. If you use RTV to seal the cover then do so sparingly. RTV boogers inside the tranny can get into things and cause problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||V6pack||02-21-2000||03:16 PM||||I did this fix using the information I found on "Oliver's" very helpful webpage.<BR>Unhooking when it is not locked up just denies you the "overdrive" you get from the direct lockup and costs you a little mileage.<P>But, when you consider it unlocks everytime you hit the brakes or pop the gas normally anyway....a short term use unplugged won't hurt anything but your mileage. Some people have cars that never lock up and they don't even know it.<P>So if you need to wait to do the fix, don't live in fear of ruining your tranny. <P>When you do the job you can put in a new filter and change fluid.  The fix took me several hours and the hardest part was plugging in the solenoid to the case.  It is in a very tough to reach place. I finally got it by standing in the "trunk", facing backwards, reaching back under with my right hand....funny now!...not then.   If I can find Oliver's site info I'll post it later for you.  Good Luck!||||reg||12
Z||000003||william_thorniii||02-21-2000||06:44 PM||||Joe,<P>To address your initial quesion... If you are trying to determine if the TC is locked during normal driving and therefore causing VERY j*e*r*k*y stops then yes, you can unplug the solenoid for diagnostic purposes.  Naturally if this does not improve your symptoms... you have the wrong tree (or something like that anyway).<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000004||Joe Trainer||02-21-2000||09:37 PM||||Thanks for the info guys.  I found Olivers post and another by Chuck Kichline who includes color pictures detailing the R&R process.  I'll be changing the solenoid, but wanted to know if any harm would occur if driven to get home in an emergency.  This is my sons car and I'll show him how to disconnect the solenoid.  Chucks address: <A HREF="http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kichline.com/chuck/fiero/TCC.htm</A> <BR>I found the info in Fiero Secrets-a great resource.||||reg||10
Z||000005||theogre||02-22-2000||11:10 AM||||It woun't hurt it in the short term at all. It'll just run higher RPM down the highway. about 200-250 more than normal with the tranny locked would.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Boxdin||02-24-2000||11:45 AM||||Correct me if I'm wrong, Ogre, but wasn't the same trans (th125)used in X-Cars from 1980 to ?? without locking torque converters. My Mom had one of those for years, the conv never locked up of course, and there was never any probs. I know of some of those cars that went 200k miles w/o probs.||||reg||6
Z||000007||Joe Trainer||02-24-2000||12:05 PM||||Just spoke with a neighbor who ownes a trans shop and he indicated that running the auto trans without the torque converter lock up solenoid connected for short periods is OK.  If disconnected for an extended period, the trans fluid will overheat (something to do with the fluid not circulating properly) and cause major problems. So if you have a problem with it, get it repaired.   Joe||||reg||3
Z||000008||terryk||02-24-2000||12:10 PM||||Only 80 and some early 81's X cars had no lock up convertor.<P>The biggest issue is that at cruise speed the lock up style convertor left unlocked gets REAL HOT. It's ok for short term testing, but I wouldn't cruise 100 miles like that at 60 MPH. It will be a durability problem not instant destruction.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||02-24-2000||07:31 PM||||that I know of all TH125c use a locker. There may have been an earlier version of the tranny without it, I haven't seen it. The earlier version would have been designed signifigantly diferantly. It's not just a matter of adding the locker tourque. It changes the whole flow system.<P>No. I wouldn't drive far without it. And if I absolutly had to I'd keep my speed down. If I had to drive a long way, more than 75-100miles, on the highway I'd take the stalling over running w/o it. then when I got local to my destination unplug it again so it doesn't die at every light. (might have to let it cool some to unstick this way.) the point is to avoid the heat as much as posible.<P>You might as well do a fluid and filter on it while you have it that far apart.||||reg||1
